;; Provisioning, and no login prompt.
;;
;; Two problems one pack solves, because both are files rather than symbols.
;;
;; The login prompt: profile:locked sets BR2_TARGET_GENERIC_GETTY=n, and a
;; boot showed the prompt anyway. That symbol governs Buildroot's own getty
;; setup; under systemd the console login is serial-getty@.service, generated
;; from systemd's presets against the console on the kernel command line, and
;; no Kconfig symbol reaches it. The overlay masks it — a symlink to /dev/null,
;; systemd's own way of saying a unit can never start — along with getty@ and
;; console-getty. Boot messages still print, which is the point: they are the
;; evidence, and they are how anyone diagnoses a board that will not come up.
;;
;; The provisioning: the appliance formats its state partition if blank,
;; generates a keypair if it has none, brings up wg0 and prints the public key
;; on the console. Nothing can connect until a peer is added, which is the one
;; step a deployment must do and the one step that cannot be done in advance.

(fragment feature:wireguard-appliance

  (doc "First-boot WireGuard provisioning on a state partition, and no console login")

  (requires
    (capability mmu)
    (capability systemd-init))

  (buildroot
    ;; blkid and mountpoint, to tell a blank disk from a provisioned one and
    ;; an already-mounted directory from an empty one. Busybox has both but
    ;; not reliably enough to depend on across configurations.
    (y BR2_PACKAGE_UTIL_LINUX)
    (y BR2_PACKAGE_UTIL_LINUX_BINARIES)

    ;; mkfs.ext4 for the state partition on first boot.
    (y BR2_PACKAGE_E2FSPROGS)

    ;; ip(8). wg-quick is deliberately not used: it is a bash script, and
    ;; bash is a shell this appliance has no reason to carry.
    (y BR2_PACKAGE_IPROUTE2)

    (path-append BR2_ROOTFS_OVERLAY "files")))
